One way to approach this problem is to use a sorting algorithm to sort the numbers in ascending order. Once the list is sorted, the kth smallest value will simply be the value at index k-1. However, this approach is not very efficient for large lists as sorting algorithms typically have a time complexity of O(n log n).

Another approach is to use the idea of partitioning. We can define a partition function that takes a list, a start index, and an end index, and partitions the list such that all the elements smaller than the pivot are on the left and all the elements greater than the pivot are on the right. We can then use this partition function to find the kth smallest value by recursively partitioning the list until we find the right partition that contains the kth smallest element.

Here's a possible implementation of this approach:

```python
def partition(nums, start, end):
    pivot = nums[end]
    i = start
    for j in range(start, end):
        if nums[j] <= pivot:
            nums[i], nums[j] = nums[j], nums[i]
            i += 1
    nums[i], nums[end] = nums[end], nums[i]
    return i

def quickselect(nums, start, end, k):
    if start < end:
        p = partition(nums, start, end)
        if k == p:
            return nums[k]
        elif k < p:
            return quickselect(nums, start, p-1, k)
        else:
            return quickselect(nums, p+1, end, k)
    else:
        return nums[k]

def solve(nums, k):
    return quickselect(nums, 0, len(nums)-1, k-1)
```

