Time complexity

Time complexity
We can solve it in O(n) time. We need to just find the first character of the needle string (the character that we are searching for) and then check if the characters match by doing a sliding window.

Correctness

Correctness
If the substring we are computing over the haystack ever matches the needle, then we return an answer that can sorta be right -- the index in the haystack where the needle starts.

The thing is that we're sliding a window and speeding along blindly! What if the search string has multiple characters in it? We can't skip any characters whatsoever, but we'll then need to also move the left pointer at the same time we check the characters and, basically, more or less calculate the same thing again in the inner loop to figure out whether it actually matches. This will make our code really slow! By "sliding a window", we mean "moving what characters we're looking at in the larger haystack to different locations to check more and more scenarios."

In our current code this would result in two branches -- one where we're thinking we found a match because the first character of the haystack and first character of the search string match, and then the check for the characters in the substring after the first match -- if they don't match, then try the next character in our haystack.

A better approach is to use KMP, which optimizes this check by removing the inner loop.


