Step 1: Understand the problem - Create a Python function that checks if an undirected graph contains a cycle. The graph is represented by nodes labeled from 0 to n-1 and edges between pairs of nodes. The graph may not be connected and can have up to 1000 nodes and 2000 edges.\
Step 2: Determine the approach - We can use Breadth-First Search (BFS) algorithm to traverse the graph and check for cycles. We will use a visited set, queue, and parent pointers to keep track of visited nodes and detect cycles.\
Step 3: Implement the BFS algorithm - We will start the BFS traversal from each unvisited node and mark its neighbors as visited if they haven't been visited yet. If we encounter an already visited node that is not our parent, then we have found a cycle in the graph.\
Step 4: Maintain sets for visited nodes and parent pointers - We will use a visited set to keep track of nodes that have been visited during the BFS traversal. We will also maintain a parent pointers map to keep track of the parent node for each visited node.\
Step 5: Check for cycles during BFS traversal - If we encounter an already visited node that is not our parent, we can conclude that there is a cycle in the graph, and we can return True. If we finish traversing the graph without finding any cycles, we can return False.\

